tomato|A vining or bushy nightshade grown for its juicy fruit, from small cherries to large beefsteaks. Needs warmth, sun and steady watering.|Fruit set fails when nights stay below about {13} or days go above {32}.
cherry_tomato|Small-fruited tomatoes that crop heavily over a long season. Easy for beginners and great in pots.|Copes better with heat and short seasons than large tomatoes.
sweet_pepper|Mild, crunchy peppers that ripen from green to red, yellow or orange. Slow to start, so they are usually raised indoors.|Slow to germinate in cool soil; give the seed tray bottom heat.
chili_pepper|Hot peppers in every shape and strength, from mild jalapeños to fiery habaneros. Compact plants that also suit pots.|Habanero and tabasco types need even more heat than other chilies.
eggplant|A heat-loving nightshade with glossy purple, white or striped fruit. Grow it in the warmest, sunniest spot you have.|Growth stalls below about {15}.
tomatillo|Husk-covered green or purple fruit used for salsa verde. Sprawling plants that crop generously in warm summers.|Plant at least two for pollination.
cape_gooseberry|Sweet-tart golden berries wrapped in papery lanterns. Easy to grow like a tomato, but bushier.|Perennial in frost-free climates.
potato|A staple tuber crop planted from seed potatoes. Early varieties are ready in about ten weeks; maincrops store all winter.|Plant when soil reaches about {7}. Frost kills the leaves but they can regrow; tubers stop forming above about {27}.
cucumber|Fast-growing vines for salads and pickling. Grows well on a trellis, which keeps fruit clean and straight.|Roots dislike disturbance; sow in pots if starting indoors.
zucchini|A prolific summer squash; one or two plants feed a family. Pick fruits small for the best texture.|Harvest young and often to keep plants producing.
winter_squash|Storage squashes such as butternut, kabocha and kuri with sweet, dense flesh. Cured fruit keeps for months.|Needs space and a long warm season.
pumpkin|Large, sprawling vines with big orange fruit for cooking, carving or storing.|Long vines; needs a long warm season.
melon|Sweet, fragrant fruit such as cantaloupe and honeydew on sprawling vines. Needs heat and sun to ripen well.|Flavour suffers in cool, wet summers.
watermelon|Large, juicy summer fruit on long vines. A crop for hot climates and long seasons.|In cool climates, choose small-fruited, early varieties.
luffa|A climbing gourd eaten young like zucchini, or left to mature into a natural sponge.|Needs a long hot season and a sturdy trellis.
bitter_melon|A climbing gourd with warty, bitter fruit popular in Asian and Indian cooking.|Harvest while still green.
chayote|A vigorous perennial vine with pear-shaped squash. One plant gives a big crop in warm climates.|Plant a whole sprouting fruit. It flowers as days shorten, so it needs a long frost-free autumn.
sweet_corn|Tall grass grown for sweet cobs eaten fresh. Wind-pollinated, so plant it in blocks.|Plant in blocks, not single rows. Super-sweet types need soil of {16} or more.
bush_bean|Compact beans that need no support and crop within two months. Pick pods often to keep them coming.|Seeds rot in cold, wet soil.
pole_bean|Climbing beans that crop over a longer season than bush types and save ground space.|Needs poles or a trellis.
runner_bean|A vigorous climbing bean with scarlet flowers and long pods. Popular in cooler summer climates.|Pod set is poor above about {27}.
lima_bean|Beans grown for large, buttery seeds eaten fresh or dried. Needs more heat than common beans.|Needs warmer soil than common beans.
cowpea|A tough, heat-loving legume grown for black-eyed peas and young pods. Thrives in poor soil.|Very tolerant of heat and drought.
yardlong_bean|A climbing bean with slender pods up to 45 cm long, used in stir-fries. Loves humid heat.|Pick pods when pencil-thin.
soybean|Grown for edamame, the green beans eaten from the pod, or for dry soybeans.|Many varieties flower as days shorten; choose ones bred for your latitude.
pea|A cool-season climber for sweet shelling peas, snap peas or snow peas. One of the first crops of spring.|Stops producing in hot weather; sow in early spring, or autumn in mild climates.
fava_bean|A hardy bean with large, meaty seeds. Grows in cool weather and needs no trellis.|Can be sown in autumn and overwintered where winters are mild.
chickpea|A drought-tolerant legume grown for its seeds, eaten fresh green or dried.|Likes dry conditions as it matures.
peanut|A legume whose pods develop underground after the flowers bury themselves in the soil.|Needs loose, sandy soil and a long warm season.
okra|A tall, heat-loving plant with hibiscus-like flowers and edible pods. Thrives where summers are hot.|Soak seed overnight. Grows very poorly below about {18}.
sweet_potato|A trailing vine grown for sweet, orange or purple roots. Needs a long, hot season.|Grown from rooted shoots called slips; plant when nights stay above about {13}.
water_spinach|A fast, semi-aquatic leafy green with hollow stems, popular in Southeast Asian cooking.|Needs constantly wet soil or shallow water. Restricted as an invasive weed in some regions.
amaranth|A heat-loving plant grown for spinach-like leaves; some types also give edible seed.|Leaves are ready in 4–7 weeks; grain types take about 100 days.
malabar_spinach|A climbing vine with thick, glossy leaves that replaces spinach in hot weather.|Give it a trellis and plenty of heat.
taro|A tropical plant grown for starchy corms and leaves, eaten cooked.|The raw plant irritates the mouth and skin; always cook it.
cassava|A tropical shrub grown for starchy roots, a staple food in many warm countries.|Grown from stem cuttings. Roots must be peeled and cooked; raw cassava is toxic.
ginger|A tropical perennial grown for its spicy rhizome. Easy to start from a fresh, plump piece.|Can be grown in pots and brought indoors for winter.
turmeric|A ginger relative grown for its bright orange rhizomes, used fresh or dried as spice.|Prefers humid heat and partial shade.
lettuce|The classic salad leaf, in loose-leaf, butterhead, romaine and crisphead types. Grows fast in cool weather.|Seed goes dormant when soil is above about {27} and plants bolt in heat. Sow shallow: seed needs light.
spinach|A fast cool-season leafy green, eaten raw or cooked. Best in spring and autumn.|Bolts quickly with long, warm days.
arugula|A fast, peppery salad green with lobed leaves, ready in a few weeks.|Turns hot and bolts in summer heat.
kale|A hardy leafy cabbage that crops for months, through autumn and winter.|Flavour improves after frost.
cabbage|Firm heads of green, red or crinkled savoy leaves. Spring, summer and winter types extend the season.|Heads split if growth surges after rain; harvest promptly.
broccoli|Grown for green flower heads; side shoots keep coming after the main head is cut.|Heads turn loose and bitter in heat.
cauliflower|Dense white, purple or green curds. Needs steady growth with cool temperatures and moisture.|The fussiest brassica; stress makes small, loose heads.
brussels_sprouts|Tall stems lined with mini cabbages, harvested from autumn into winter.|Sprouts taste best after cool autumn weather and frost.
kohlrabi|A cabbage relative with a swollen, crunchy stem eaten raw or cooked. Quick and easy.|Harvest at tennis-ball size before it turns woody.
collards|Large, smooth leaves on tall stems, picked over a long season. Copes with heat and cold.|More heat tolerant than other brassicas.
bok_choy|A fast Asian cabbage with crisp white stems and dark leaves, great for stir-fries.|Bolts with long days and temperature swings; easiest sown in late summer.
napa_cabbage|An oblong Chinese cabbage with tender, crinkled leaves, the base of kimchi.|Best sown after midsummer to avoid bolting.
mizuna|A mild, feathery-leaved Japanese green for salads and stir-fries.|Cut leaves and they regrow several times.
mustard_greens|Spicy leaves in green or deep red, eaten young in salads or cooked when larger.|Leaves get spicier in warm weather.
turnip|A quick root crop with white flesh; the leafy tops are edible too.|Harvest roots small for the sweetest flavour.
rutabaga|A large, yellow-fleshed root that keeps well through winter.|Sweetest when it matures in cold weather.
radish|The fastest root crop, ready in a month. Crisp, peppery roots in many colours.|Turns pithy and hot in heat; sow small batches often.
daikon|A large, mild white radish for cooking, pickling and loosening heavy soil.|Sow in late summer; spring sowings tend to bolt.
carrot|A sweet root in orange, purple, yellow or white. Grows best in loose, stone-free soil.|Slow to germinate (1–3 weeks); keep the seedbed moist.
beet|Sweet roots in red, gold or striped, with tasty leaves too.|Each seed is a cluster; thin the seedlings.
swiss_chard|Large leaves on colourful stems, picked over many months. Tolerates heat better than spinach.|Pick outer leaves and the plant keeps growing.
parsnip|A long, sweet, cream-coloured root, harvested from autumn through winter.|Use fresh seed; roots sweeten after frost.
celery|Crisp stalks for soups and salads. Needs a long season and plenty of water.|Long cool spells below {10} on young plants cause bolting.
celeriac|A knobbly root with celery flavour, great in soups and mash.|Very long season; start indoors early.
fennel|Grown for its crisp, aniseed-flavoured bulb, eaten raw or roasted.|Bolts if sown too early in spring or stressed; summer sowing is most reliable.
leek|A hardy, mild member of the onion family that stands in the ground all winter.|Plant deep to grow long white stems.
onion|A kitchen staple grown from seed or small sets. Bulbs store for months.|Bulbs form according to day length: short-day types near the equator, long-day types at high latitudes. Sets are easiest.
spring_onion|Slim, mild onions that never form a bulb. Ready in about two months.|Sow every few weeks for a steady supply.
shallot|Small, mild onions that multiply into clusters, each bulb giving several more.|Plant bulbs in late winter, or in autumn where winters are mild.
garlic|Planted as single cloves that grow into full bulbs. Hardneck and softneck types suit different climates.|Plant cloves in autumn; needs about 6 weeks below {10} to form proper bulbs. In warm climates, chill cloves in the fridge first.
asparagus|A long-lived perennial whose spring shoots are a delicacy. A bed can crop for 15+ years.|Plant dormant crowns in early spring; first light harvest in year 2–3.
rhubarb|A hardy perennial grown for tart stalks used in pies and jams.|The leaves are poisonous; eat only the stalks. Struggles where winters stay warm.
artichoke|A large, thistle-like perennial grown for its edible flower buds.|Perennial in mild climates; elsewhere grown as an annual from seed started early.
jerusalem_artichoke|A tall sunflower relative with knobbly, nutty tubers. Very easy and very productive.|Spreads aggressively; contain it.
endive|A slightly bitter salad green with curly or broad leaves.|Blanching the heart reduces the bitterness.
radicchio|A chicory with red, bitter leaves that sweeten in cold weather. Lovely grilled.|Sow in midsummer; heads colour up in cool autumn weather.
lambs_lettuce|A small, nutty salad green that grows through the coldest months.|Classic winter salad; sow from late summer to autumn.
watercress|A peppery aquatic green that grows in running water or very wet soil.|Needs constantly wet soil or running water.
miners_lettuce|A hardy winter salad with round, succulent leaves.|Germinates poorly above about {15}.
purslane|A succulent, lemony salad green that loves heat and drought.|Rich in omega-3 fatty acids.
salsify|A slow root crop with a mild, oyster-like flavour.|Needs deep, loose soil for long roots.
scorzonera|A black-skinned root vegetable with white flesh, harvested in winter.|Roots bleed latex when cut; scrub rather than peel.
horseradish|A tough perennial grown for its fiery white root.|Plant root cuttings in spring; very invasive.
parsley|The most-used kitchen herb, with curly or flat leaves. Crops for months.|Germination is slow (2–4 weeks); soak seed overnight.
coriander|A fast herb grown for fresh leaves (cilantro) and spicy seeds.|Bolts fast in heat and long days; sow every 3 weeks.
dill|A feathery herb for fish, pickles and salads. Flowers attract helpful insects.|Taprooted; sow where it will grow.
chervil|A delicate herb with a mild aniseed taste, one of the French fines herbes.|Prefers shade and cool weather; bolts in summer heat.
chives|A hardy perennial with mild onion-flavoured leaves and edible purple flowers.|Easiest bought as a plant or divided from a clump.
garlic_chives|Flat-leaved chives with a garlic flavour and white flowers.|Self-seeds readily.
basil|A fragrant, tender herb that loves heat. Pinch off flowers to keep leaves coming.|Leaves blacken below about {10}; wait for warm nights.
thai_basil|A spicy, aniseed-scented basil with purple stems, used in Southeast Asian cooking.|More heat tolerant than sweet basil.
holy_basil|A sacred, clove-scented basil from India, used in tea and cooking.|Perennial in the tropics.
mint|A vigorous perennial for tea, sauces and drinks, with many flavours.|Extremely invasive; grow it in a pot.
thyme|A low, woody Mediterranean herb with tiny aromatic leaves.|Hates wet roots; needs sharp drainage.
rosemary|An evergreen Mediterranean shrub with needle-like, resinous leaves.|Seed germinates poorly; buy plants or take cuttings. Bring indoors where winters drop below about {-7}.
sage|A shrubby herb with soft grey-green leaves, classic with roast dishes.|Replace woody plants every 4–5 years.
oregano|A spreading Mediterranean herb, essential for pizza and tomato dishes.|Seed needs light to germinate; press it onto the surface.
marjoram|A sweet, gentle relative of oregano.|Grown as an annual in cold climates.
tarragon|A perennial herb with a strong aniseed flavour, key to béarnaise sauce.|True French tarragon sets no viable seed; buy plants. Seed sold as tarragon is the milder Russian type.
lemon_balm|A lemon-scented perennial in the mint family, lovely for tea.|Self-seeds freely.
lovage|A tall perennial that tastes like strong celery.|One plant is plenty.
summer_savory|A peppery annual herb, traditionally cooked with beans.|A traditional companion to beans.
lemongrass|A tropical grass with citrus-scented stalks used in Asian cooking.|Root stalks from the grocery store in water; overwinter indoors in cold climates.
stevia|A small shrub whose leaves are intensely sweet, used as a sugar substitute.|Seed germinates erratically; plants are more reliable.
borage|A bristly annual with star-shaped blue flowers that bees love. Flowers are edible.|Self-seeds.
chamomile|A daisy-flowered annual whose flowers make a calming tea.|Needs light to germinate; scatter seed on the surface.
fenugreek|A quick legume grown for aromatic leaves (methi) and seeds.|Leaves are ready in about 30 days, seeds in about 100.
culantro|A tropical herb with a strong coriander flavour and long, serrated leaves.|Prefers shade and humidity; bolts in full sun.
lavender|A fragrant Mediterranean shrub with purple spikes loved by bees.|Seed benefits from 3–4 weeks of cold. Needs sharp drainage.
strawberry|Low perennial plants with sweet red berries. Summer-fruiting and everbearing types extend the season.|Summer-fruiting types crop the year after planting; everbearing types within about 3 months. Replace plants every 3–4 years.
raspberry|Cane fruit with soft red berries in summer or autumn, depending on the variety.|Plant dormant canes from late autumn to early spring.
blackberry|Vigorous cane fruit with glossy black berries in late summer.|Thornless varieties are far easier to manage.
blueberry|A bushy shrub with sweet blue berries and fiery autumn leaves.|Needs acidic soil (pH 4.5–5.5). Low-chill varieties exist for warm climates.
red_currant|A compact bush with strings of glossy, tart red berries.|Tolerates partial shade. Black currants are grown the same way.
gooseberry|A spiny bush with tart green or red berries for jams and desserts.|Struggles in hot summers.
grape|A long-lived climbing vine for table grapes, juice or wine.|Plant dormant vines in early spring; first crop in year 2–3.
apple|The classic fruit tree, with thousands of varieties for eating, cooking and cider.|Most varieties need a pollination partner and winter cold. Plant bare-root trees while dormant.
pear|A long-lived fruit tree with juicy European or crisp Asian pears.|Needs a pollination partner.
plum|A fruit tree with sweet plums for eating fresh, baking or jam.|Many European plums are self-fertile.
cherry|A fruit tree with sweet dark or bicolour cherries in early summer.|Needs plenty of winter cold; net against birds.
peach|A fruit tree with juicy peaches or nectarines for warm summers.|Early flowers are vulnerable to spring frost. Apricots are grown the same way.
fig|A Mediterranean tree or bush with soft, sweet fruit. Grows well in pots.|Protect the roots in cold winters.
olive|A slow-growing evergreen tree for olives and oil, typical of Mediterranean climates.|Needs some winter cool to flower, but is damaged below about {-9}.
pomegranate|A shrub or small tree with jewel-like seeds and bright red flowers.|Needs hot, dry summers to ripen fully.
kiwifruit|A vigorous climbing vine with fuzzy kiwis; needs strong support.|Needs male and female plants. Hardy kiwi survives down to about {-30}.
lemon|A small evergreen citrus tree that flowers and fruits for much of the year.|In cold climates, grow it in a large pot and bring it indoors for winter.
orange|An evergreen citrus tree with sweet oranges or mandarins.|Mandarins tolerate slightly more cold.
avocado|A large subtropical tree with rich, buttery fruit.|Grafted trees fruit in 3–4 years; seedlings may take 10 or more.
mango|A large tropical tree with sweet, fragrant fruit.|Needs a distinct dry season to flower well.
banana|A giant tropical herb with large leaves and bunches of fruit.|Plant suckers or nursery plants; growth stops below about {14}.
papaya|A fast, single-stemmed tropical tree that fruits within a year from seed.|Plant several, as many trees are male.
passion_fruit|A vigorous vine with exotic flowers and tangy, aromatic fruit.|Needs a strong trellis.
pineapple|A spiky tropical plant that grows one fruit from its centre.|Grow it from a twisted-off fruit top.
dragon_fruit|A climbing cactus with night-blooming flowers and bright pink fruit.|Grown from cuttings; needs a post to climb.
guava|A small tropical tree with fragrant, vitamin-rich fruit.|Can become invasive in tropical regions.
sunflower|Tall annuals with huge yellow flower heads and edible seeds.|Sow where it will grow; it dislikes transplanting.
calendula|A cheerful orange or yellow pot marigold with edible petals.|Flowers best in cool weather and blooms into winter in mild climates.
nasturtium|A trailing annual with peppery edible leaves and bright flowers.|Rich soil gives leaves rather than flowers.
french_marigold|A compact marigold with orange, yellow and red flowers all summer.|A popular companion plant against pests.
african_marigold|A tall marigold with big pompom flowers in yellow and orange.|Taller and slower to flower than French marigold.
zinnia|Bright, long-lasting flowers for cutting, in nearly every colour.|Prone to mildew in humid shade.
cosmos|Airy, daisy-like flowers in pink, white and crimson that bloom for months.|Thrives in poor soil; too much fertiliser gives leaves.
sweet_pea|A climbing annual with highly fragrant flowers for cutting.|Not edible: the seeds are toxic. Fades in summer heat.
cornflower|A classic blue meadow flower with edible petals.|Autumn sowing gives stronger plants where winters allow.
pansy|Low, cheerful flowers with "faces" that bloom in cool weather, even in winter.|Edible flowers. Seed germinates best in darkness.
snapdragon|Spikes of dragon-mouthed flowers in many colours, great for cutting.|Seed needs light; sow on the surface.
petunia|A free-flowering annual for pots, baskets and borders.|Tiny seed; sow on the surface under light.
impatiens|Bushy plants covered in flowers, one of the few annuals that bloom in shade.|Keep evenly moist.
alyssum|Low carpets of tiny, honey-scented flowers.|Attracts hoverflies, which eat aphids.
california_poppy|Silky orange poppies that thrive in poor, dry soil.|Sow where it will grow; the taproot dislikes transplanting.
cleome|Tall, airy spider flowers in pink, purple and white.|Seeds germinate better after a week of chilling.
celosia|Velvety plumes or crests in vivid red, orange and pink.|Young leaves are eaten as a vegetable in West Africa.
nigella|Delicate blue flowers in a mist of fine leaves, followed by decorative seed pods.|Self-seeds.
larkspur|Tall spikes of blue, pink or white flowers for cottage gardens.|All parts are poisonous. Needs cool soil to germinate.
hollyhock|Very tall spires of large flowers, a cottage-garden classic.|Flowers in its second year. Edible flowers.
lupin|Stately perennial with colourful flower spikes in early summer.|Garden lupin seeds are toxic. Soak or nick seeds before sowing.
echinacea|A tough prairie perennial with pink, cone-centred flowers loved by pollinators.|Seed benefits from cold; often flowers in year two.
dahlia|Summer-to-autumn flowers in every size and form, grown from tubers.|Plant tubers after the last frost; lift and store them over winter in cold climates.
gladiolus|Tall flower spikes grown from corms, ideal for cutting.|Plant corms every 2 weeks for a long season.
tulip|Spring-flowering bulbs in almost every colour.|Plant bulbs in autumn once soil is below about {13}. Needs 12–16 weeks of cold; pre-chill bulbs in warm climates.
daffodil|Cheerful spring bulbs that come back year after year.|Plant bulbs in autumn. All parts are poisonous.
geranium|Long-flowering pelargoniums for pots, window boxes and beds.|Overwinter indoors in cold climates; easy from cuttings.
morning_glory|A fast climber with trumpet flowers that open each morning.|Seeds are toxic. Soak or nick seeds before sowing.
phacelia|Lavender-blue flowers adored by bees, also grown as green manure.|One of the best plants for bees.
